  [38;5;153mtrack(eventName, payload)   // build a record, append to the queue[39m
  [38;5;153mgetEvents()                 // read the queue (for debugging and tests)[39m
  [38;5;153mflush(sender)               // drain the queue through a sender you supply[39m
  [38;5;153mclear()                     // drop the queue[39m

  The queue lives in [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m under a single key. [38;5;153mtrack()[39m is synchronous and never throws — more on that in Section
  3. [38;5;153mflush()[39m takes the sender as an argument rather than knowing a URL, which is the seam that lets the analytics
  backend arrive later without touching any call site. Until then nothing ships anywhere; records simply accumulate and
  are inspectable in devtools.

  [1mChanged: [38;5;153mindex.html[0m — [38;5;153m<script src="app.js">[39m becomes [38;5;153m<script type="module" src="app.js">[39m.

  [1mChanged: [38;5;153mapp.js[0m — imports [38;5;153mtrack[39m, and [38;5;153mlogin()[39m calls it. [38;5;153mlogin()[39m keeps its current [38;5;153m(username, password)[39m signature. No
  [38;5;153muserId[39m parameter: nothing can supply one, and the tracker records the identity [38;5;153mlogin()[39m already has. When the real API
  lands and returns a server-side user ID, that ID gets added to the record inside [38;5;153mlogin()[39m — still no new parameter.

  [1mUntouched: [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m, [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m.[0m These are the Node/CommonJS side and nothing connects them to the browser code.
  Bridging those two worlds is a separate question and not in scope here.

  [1mThe one consequence to accept:[0m [38;5;153mindex.html[39m will no longer work opened directly as a file — native ES modules are
  blocked over [38;5;153mfile://[39m. You'll need [38;5;153mpython3 -m http.server[39m or equivalent. That's inherent to A, not to my design of it.

  [1mTooling.[0m This repo has no linter, no formatter, and no test framework. Setting those up is cheapest now, before the
  code exists, and the tracker is the first thing here that's actually worth testing — queue capping, the privacy
  allowlist, and flush-failure behavior all have edge cases that are tedious to verify by hand. Node's built-in
  [38;5;153mnode:test[39m runner needs no dependencies at all, which fits a repo with an empty [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m.
